[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    159[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Two configurations that are undefined behaviour in the C original
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    160[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    161[0m:[38;5;10m[49m These are exercised only in the sub-shape where the C code is well defined, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    162[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the restriction is documented at the test site:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    163[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    164[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`mode >= 2` + delete of a non-last element** (rows 39–40, 43). For
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    165[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `mode != STBDS_HM_STRING` the C re-find after the swap-in passes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    166[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `(char *) a + elemsize*old_index` — the *element*, not the key — to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    167[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `stbds_hm_find_slot`, which then hashes it *as a NUL-terminated string* and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    168[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strcmp`s against wild pointers. It segfaults in both implementations.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    169[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   Deletes with `mode >= 2` are therefore driven in last-element order only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    170[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`old_index == final_index`, no re-find), which still covers the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    171[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `mode == STBDS_HM_STRING` inequality that decides whether the strdup'd key is
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    172[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   freed.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    173[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`STBDS_SH_NONE` table + `mode >= 1`** (row 28). `hmput_key` memcpy's the raw
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    174[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   key bytes, but any *lookup* interprets those bytes as a `char *` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    175[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   dereferences them. Only distinct keys are inserted so no hash match occurs.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    176[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    177[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### Harness notes
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    178[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    179[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `dlopen` returns the same library instance to every caller, so the two `.so`s'
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    180[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `stbds_hash_seed` globals are shared across `#[test]` threads. Every scenario
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    181[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   runs under `common::SERIAL` and calls `stbds_rand_seed` first; without this,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    182[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   parallel tests desynchronise each other's seed LCG.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    183[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Digests are address-free. Heap pointers (`storage`, `string.storage`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    184[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `hash_table`, stored `char *` keys) are compared by *content* or by
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    185[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   *position*, never by value. `temp_key` in particular is uninitialised
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    186[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `realloc` memory until a string-mode `hmput_key` writes it, and points at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    187[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   freed memory after a `STBDS_SH_STRDUP` delete, so it is compared as "index of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    188[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   the live element whose key pointer it aliases, or -1" and only immediately
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    189[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   after a string-mode put.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    190[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * `scripts/mutation_check.sh` injects 10 small behavioural mutations into
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    191[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `src/lib.rs` and asserts the suite fails for each. Current result: **10 / 10
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    192[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   caught**.
